<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sengoku period</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sengoku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sengoku period, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, was an era of almost continuous civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from the late fifteenth to the late sixteenth century. Regional lords fought for power while central authority collapsed. Portuguese traders arrived during this period and introduced firearms, as accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> note, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> emerged from its eventual reunification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sengoku-period.jpg" alt="Sengoku period" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sengoku_period_battle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Emperor was officially the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, but he was largely a marginalised, ceremonial and religious figure who delegated power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. From 1346 to 1358 the Ashikaga shogunate expanded the authority of the shugo, local military and police officials, and the first shugo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> appeared. The sixth shogun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshinori">Ashikaga Yoshinori</a>, was assassinated in the Kakitsu Rebellion, and the shogunate gradually lost control over the daimyo.</p><p>Historians choose different starting points: the Kyōtoku incident of 1454, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> of 1467 or the Meiō incident of 1493. The Ōnin War, between an Eastern Army under Hosokawa Katsumoto and a Western Army under Yamana Sōzen, grew from a dispute over the succession to the shogun Ashikaga Yoshimasa. It lasted until 1477 and devastated two thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Daimyo then returned to their provinces, where their deputies, the shugodai, sometimes seized power or declared independence. The sengoku daimyo, unlike the earlier shugo daimyo, ruled their regions on their own, without appointment by the shogun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The period saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> rebel against their lords, succession disputes within noble families and the installation of figurehead lords. People of other social strata sometimes made names as warriors and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-soun">Hōjō Sōun</a> was the first common samurai to rise to the rank of sengoku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, a shugodai, replaced his lord. Peasants, craftsmen and merchants rose in uprisings called ikki. In the Kaga Rebellion in 1488, with the support of the True Pure Land sect, an ikki took control of the whole province of Kaga.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> the Hosokawa clan made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> a puppet after the Meiō incident, and the Miyoshi clan later rose, until the 13th shogun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a>, was killed. Elsewhere Mōri Motonari pacified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chugoku-region">Chūgoku region</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> in the Kantō and Chūbu regions, including five battles at Kawanakajima between 1553 and 1564. When the Portuguese brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> gun to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1543, the tanegashima was soon mass-produced. Standing armies of ashigaru foot soldiers became essential, small local lords could no longer remain independent, and land passed to sengoku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> with vast territories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>There is no agreed end date. Candidates include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>'s march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1568, the abolition of the Muromachi shogunate in 1573, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s law of 1587 prohibiting war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600, the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> in 1603 and the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a> in 1638. Modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> recognises Oda Nobunaga, Toyotomi Hideyoshi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> as the three great unifiers, for restoring central government. Hideyoshi's rise from a peasant background to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, sengoku <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sessho-and-kampaku">kampaku</a> is often considered the most spectacular of the era.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sengoku period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Japan, Portuguese traders who landed on Tanegashima in 1543 introduced the arquebus, which Japanese gunsmiths rapidly copied and which transformed the warfare of the <b>Sengoku period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ieyasu came to power more patiently than any other leader of the violent <b>Sengoku</b> era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Onin War of 1467 to 1477 shattered shogunal authority and began the <b>Sengoku</b> era of warring lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate</a></li><li><blockquote>Iga, a hilly province southeast of Kyoto, had no strong governor in the <b>Sengoku period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/iga-confederacy">Iga confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ujichika expanded into Totomi and issued one of the earliest <b>Sengoku</b> house codes, the Imagawa kana mokuroku of 1526.</blockquote><a href="/state/imagawa-clan-domain">Imagawa clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hojo carried out land surveys, registered vassals by their military obligations and simplified taxes, building one of the most systematic <b>Sengoku</b> administrations.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
